well um the key takeaways are that the  united states and the united kingdom  share so much in common we share values  we share history  we share  a similar outlook  in terms of  the challenges that we face in today's  global economy um and i think we share  a lot of interests and opportunities so  i'm delighted to have been here visiting  scotland and london on the second part  of the  dialogues on the future of atlantic  trade and  where this takes us  i'm really open-minded about where this  takes us i'm extremely optimistic about  opportunities that we have to  collaborate  to expand upon everything that we share  in order to deliver uh meaningful  concrete  results for our workers um our  businesses our communities and our  economies  i mean such positive uh rhetoric there  and clearly lots of meetings that that  keep taking place i'm sure you'll be  aware that for some people in the uk  that that holy grail of a full free  trade deal with the us  appears to be quite elusive and  relatively delayed you know years after  the brexit has taken place is that your  perspective as well would you like to  get to that full free trade deal fairly  soon well let me say a little bit about  my perspective which is what we do in  trade and economics  has to make sense for our economies and  our people  with respect to the partners that we  work with but also with respect to the  challenges that we're facing the global  economy that we are living in today is  significantly different from the one  just  two and a half years ago in that time  we've had significant  public health crisis followed by an  economic crisis and supply chain  disruptions caused by the covid pandemic  and just in the last two months we've  had russia's invasion of ukraine which  in addition to being a foreign policy  crisis national security crisis is also  going to have economic impacts that we  are all going to have to deal with and  how important are those extra aspects of  factors that you have in 